2. Mathematical Foundations

A candidate must command a tool‑chain that enables every stage of the governance‑compiler pipeline, from formal specification to verified deployment, to physical‑world calibration.

2.1 Analysis and Probability
Real analysis, measure theory, and measure‑theoretic probability (construction of stochastic processes, weak convergence, martingales); rigorous ODEs/PDEs (existence, uniqueness, stability, Lyapunov functions); large deviations theory and concentration inequalities (Hoeffding, Bernstein, McDiarmid) for sample‑complexity bounds.

2.2 Dynamics and Stochastic Processes
Continuous‑time Markov chains, Poisson processes, diffusion processes, and stochastic differential equations; dynamical systems theory (phase portraits, bifurcations, centre manifolds, Lyapunov’s direct method); the replicator equation and adaptive dynamics:

\dot{x}_i = x_i \bigl( f_i(\mathbf{x}) - \bar{f}(\mathbf{x}) \bigr),

where f_i is the fitness of type i and \bar{f} is the mean fitness.

2.3 Game Theory and Mechanism Design
Non‑cooperative game theory (Nash equilibrium, correlated equilibrium, extensive‑form games); mechanism design (dominant‑strategy incentive compatibility, Vickrey–Clarke–Groves mechanisms, the revelation principle); evolutionary game theory (evolutionarily stable strategies, stochastic stability in finite populations); computational social choice (Arrow’s and Gibbard–Satterthwaite theorems, fair division and voting algorithms).

2.4 Networks and Complexity
Graph theory (Laplacian matrices, spectral clustering, centrality, percolation thresholds); information theory (entropy, mutual information, channel capacity, the Shannon–Hartley theorem) applied to delayed communication governance; control theory for networked systems (passivity, small‑gain theorem, stability under feedback delay).

2.5 Formal Logic and Verification
Propositional and first‑order logic; linear temporal logic (LTL) and computation tree logic (CTL); model checking (explicit‑state and symbolic SAT/SMT‑based algorithms); interactive theorem proving (type theory, Curry–Howard correspondence, working proficiency in Coq, Lean, or Isabelle/HOL); category theory for compositional modelling (symmetric monoidal categories, operads, wiring diagrams as a language for open, interconnected institutional components).

