7. Adversarial Validation and the Governance Sandbox

7.1 Comprehensive Exam as a Red‑Teaming Competition

The comprehensive examination is a public adversarial defence. The candidate submits a formal governance kernel; the committee, or competing student teams, act as adversarial agents tasked with inducing system collapse using only legally valid actions. The candidate passes only by producing a machine‑checked proof, via SMT solver or proof assistant, that no sequence of moves within the defined action space reaches a forbidden state.

7.2 Governance Sandbox — The Hub‑and‑Spoke Implementation Model

The Sandbox is the empirical spine of the program, integrated through a Hub‑and‑Spoke design that combines partnered analog sites with a dedicated on‑campus testbed.

Approach Role of PhD Candidate Primary Value
Partnered Analogs (Spokes) Experimental Observer Validates the model against pre‑existing, high‑stress organisational hierarchies that the program did not design
Dedicated Testbed (Hub) Chief System Designer Full experimental control; enables A/B testing of governance rules and deliberate adversarial stress‑testing

7.2.1 The Institutional Stress Lab (“Micro‑Polity Facility”)

A modular, 10–20 person on‑campus facility built for 4‑week rotations, not a full space‑station analog. Required design specifications:

· Sensor Saturation: Privacy‑compliant, continuous instrumentation of communication, movement, and resource‑allocation events, generating the time series used to calibrate Tier‑2 cognitive agents.
· The Governance UI: A physical interface through which participants exercise the institution: voting, resource requests, sanction reporting.
· The Fail‑Safe System: A human‑in‑the‑loop override protocol permitting an emergency reset of governance parameters if the test threatens physical or psychological well‑being.

7.2.2 Adversarial Governance Runs

The Hub’s controlled setting allows the deliberate seeding of “agent provocateurs”—research assistants trained to exhibit Tier‑2/Tier‑3 exploit‑seeking behaviour—to test whether the institution holds under manufactured stress, directly generating empirical counterparts to the P1–P7 proof obligations.

7.2.3 Participant Recruitment — Incentivised Cohorts

· The “Academy of Colonists” Program: Graduate students in sociology, engineering, and psychology recruited as a prestigious, credit‑bearing field‑research internship.
· Performance Incentives (“Governance Bounties”): Compensation tied to collective colony success (sustained resource reserves, completed legislative cycles), structurally aligning participant incentives with the institution’s own objective function.
· Gamified Realism (“Mission Readiness Simulation”): Framing the residency as crew training for a lunar mission, supplying the psychological seriousness the governance rules require to be tested honestly.

7.2.4 Ethical and Regulatory Framing

To operate without recurring IRB blockage, the sandbox is framed as a System Engineering Safety Trial, not a psychological experiment:

· Informed Consent via Constitutional Contract: Participants sign a “Mission Charter” that functions simultaneously as legal waiver and as their agreement to the simulation’s specific governance rules.
· The Kill‑Switch Governance Role: An external System Auditor, not embedded in the sandbox, holds independent authority to pause a run if institutional‑stress metrics exceed pre‑registered bounds.
· Transparency as Default: The Governance UI exposes a real‑time Health Dashboard to participants themselves, so institutional health becomes a shared, visible burden rather than a variable hidden from the population being governed.

7.2.5 Frozen Rules vs. Iterative Constitutional Evolution

Every 4‑week Hub residency is split into two governed phases, rather than choosing one mode program‑wide:

· Phase A — Frozen Baseline (Weeks 1–2). The institutional ruleset is locked. This isolates the variable the dissertation’s proof portfolio addresses: does this specific, formally verified kernel survive contact with real bounded‑rational and adversarial humans. Any deviation between predicted and observed behaviour is attributed to the agent model, not to the institution changing—these data recalibrate Tier‑2 parameters.
· Phase B — Governed Iteration (Weeks 3–4). Constitutional amendment is unlocked, but only through the same formally specified amendment procedure encoded in the Institutional Grammar Layer (Section 3)—amendments must themselves type‑check and re‑satisfy the P1–P7 obligations before taking effect inside the sandbox. This phase tests whether the amendment process itself is stable, or whether it becomes a vector for institutional collapse.

Running both phases in sequence, on the same cohort, turns “frozen vs. iterative” from an either/or design choice into two separately falsifiable claims the dissertation must defend: one about the institution, one about the institution’s capacity to safely change itself.

7.2.6 Implementation Roadmap

1. Year 1 (Pilot): Partnerships with 2–3 existing analog facilities; doctoral candidates deployed as Governance Observers to catalogue naturally occurring failure modes.
2. Year 2 (Development): Construction of the on‑campus Governance Testbed, with primary engineering effort on the Governance UI/UX.
3. Year 3 (Integration): Mandatory 4‑week doctoral residencies begin; Year 1–2 data calibrates the Tier‑2 cognitive agents in the master simulation engine.

