Appendix A. Formal Semantics of the Institutional Grammar Layer

The Institutional Grammar Layer is a dependently typed domain‑specific language embedded in a constructive type theory (e.g. the Calculus of Inductive Constructions as implemented in Coq or Lean). Its operational semantics are given by a labelled transition system over the state space S.

A.1 Abstract Syntax

An institution is a tuple

\mathcal{I} = (S, A, T, R, \Phi)

where

· S is the set of institutional states, each a record of resource levels, agent roles, and ongoing legislative procedures;
· A = \prod_{i=1}^{N} A_i is the product of action spaces of the N agents (human, AI, or mixed);
· T: S \times A \to S is the deterministic state‑transition function;
· R: S \times A \to \mathbb{R}^N is the reward (or payoff) schedule;
· \Phi: S \times A \to \mathbb{R}^N is the sanction schedule (non‑positive).

The type system enforces that every rule carries a pre‑condition Pre : S → Prop and a post‑condition Post : S → A → S → Prop. An action a is legal in state s only if Pre(s) is provable. The transition is then constrained to states s' such that Post(s, a, s') is provable. In the dependently typed setting, the transition function has type

T : \prod_{s:S} \left( \sum_{a:A} \texttt{Pre}(s) \right) \to \left\{ s':S \mid \texttt{Post}(s,a,s') \right\}.

A.2 Operational Semantics

A run of the institution is an infinite sequence

s_0 \xrightarrow{a_0} s_1 \xrightarrow{a_1} s_2 \xrightarrow{a_2} \cdots

where each a_t is legal in s_t and s_{t+1} = T(s_t, a_t). The set of reachable states \text{Reach}(\mathcal{I}) \subseteq S is defined inductively.

Theorem A.1 (Type Soundness).
If \mathcal{I} is well‑typed and the initial state s_0 satisfies a global safety invariant \text{Inv}: S \to \text{Prop} (i.e. \texttt{Inv}(s_0) is provable), and every rule preserves \text{Inv} (Post implies \texttt{Inv}(s')), then every reachable state satisfies \text{Inv}. That is, \forall s \in \text{Reach}(\mathcal{I}),\; \texttt{Inv}(s).

Proof sketch. By induction on the length of the run. The base case holds by assumption. For the inductive step, if s_t satisfies \text{Inv} and a_t is legal, the rule’s post‑condition guarantees \text{Inv}(s_{t+1}). ∎

This theorem is the foundation for the seamless compilation from specification to verification: safety invariants are merely type‑checked obligations.

