Appendix B. Proof Portfolio — Formal Statements and Proof Sketches

Every doctoral dissertation must include machine‑checked proofs (in Coq, Lean, or Isabelle/HOL) of the following seven properties. Sketches of the underlying mathematical arguments are provided here; the candidate must produce fully formalised versions.

B.1 Incentive Compatibility (IC)

Definition B.1 (Dominant‑strategy incentive compatibility).
A mechanism (A, R) is dominant‑strategy incentive compatible (DSIC) if for every agent i, every true preference (type) \theta_i, every possible misreport \theta'_i, and every profile of others’ reports \theta_{-i},

\mathbb{E}\bigl[ R_i( f(\theta_i, \theta_{-i}) ) \mid \theta_i \bigr] \ge \mathbb{E}\bigl[ R_i( f(\theta'_i, \theta_{-i}) ) \mid \theta_i \bigr],

where f is the social choice function implemented by the mechanism.

Theorem B.1 (VCG mechanism for resource allocation).
Consider a lunar colony with K divisible resources and quasi‑linear agent utilities v_i(x_i) - p_i. The Vickrey–Clarke–Groves mechanism, which allocates resources to maximise reported total value and charges each agent the externality they impose, is DSIC.

Proof sketch. Standard result from mechanism design. The candidate must instantiate the general VCG formula to the specific resource allocation problem of the colony, formalise the proof in a proof assistant, and verify that the mechanism respects the institutional grammar’s pre‑ and post‑conditions. ∎

For non‑quasi‑linear settings, the candidate may prove a weaker notion (e.g. Bayesian incentive compatibility) and quantify the worst‑case manipulation gain.

B.2 Structural Stability (Lyapunov)

Theorem B.2 (Local asymptotic stability of a cooperative equilibrium).
Let \mathbf{x} \in \Delta^{m-1} be the population vector of m behavioural strategies, evolving under the replicator‑mutator dynamics

\dot{x}_i = x_i \bigl( f_i(\mathbf{x}) - \bar{f}(\mathbf{x}) \bigr) + \sum_j \mu_{ji} x_j - \mu_{ij} x_i,

where \mu_{ij} are mutation rates. Assume that the cooperative equilibrium \mathbf{x}^* is a strict Nash equilibrium when \mu = 0. Then there exists a neighbourhood U of \mathbf{x}^* and a continuously differentiable function V: U \to \mathbb{R} such that V(\mathbf{x}^*) = 0, V(\mathbf{x}) > 0 for \mathbf{x} \neq \mathbf{x}^*, and \dot{V}(\mathbf{x}) < 0 for all \mathbf{x} \in U \setminus \{\mathbf{x}^*\}.

Proof sketch. Construct the Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{x}) = -\sum_{i \in \text{coop}} \ln x_i (a standard choice for replicator dynamics). Compute its time derivative using the dynamics and the strictness of the equilibrium to show negativity near \mathbf{x}^*. The candidate must extend this to include stochastic perturbations (SDE) and prove stochastic stability using a Lyapunov–Foster criterion. ∎

B.3 Safety Invariants (Linear Temporal Logic)

Theorem B.3 (Global safety: no resource depletion).
Consider the Kripke structure \mathcal{K} whose states are the reachable institutional states S and whose transition relation is T. Let \varphi_{\text{forbidden}} be the atomic proposition “water reserve R_w \le 0”. The candidate must prove

\mathcal{K} \models \Box \neg \varphi_{\text{forbidden}}.

Proof sketch. The proof proceeds by exhibiting an inductive invariant I such that (i) I(s_0) holds, (ii) for any transition s \to s', I(s) \land \text{legal}(s,a) \Rightarrow I(s'), and (iii) I(s) \Rightarrow R_w(s) > 0. The candidate encodes the institution’s rules in the modelling language of a model checker (e.g. NuSMV) or directly in a proof assistant and verifies the implications. For infinite state spaces (continuous resources), the candidate uses predicate abstraction or an SMT‑based invariant generation tool such as Z3 with k-induction. ∎

B.4 Convergence of Adaptive Governance Algorithms

Theorem B.4 (Convergence to Pareto‑optimal configuration).
Let the institution employ an online learning algorithm that updates the resource allocation rule parameters \theta_t according to a projected stochastic gradient ascent on a social welfare function W(\theta) = \sum_i u_i(\theta). If W is concave and the step sizes satisfy \sum_{t=1}^\infty \eta_t = \infty,\; \sum_{t=1}^\infty \eta_t^2 < \infty, then \theta_t converges almost surely to the set of Pareto‑optimal parameters \Theta^*.

Proof sketch. This is a standard stochastic approximation result (Robbins–Monro). The candidate must verify the assumptions (bounded gradients, unbiased estimates) in the institutional setting and prove, using a Lyapunov argument or the ODE method, that the limit set of \theta_t is a subset of \Theta^*. ∎

If a finite‑time convergence guarantee is required, the candidate may prove an iteration complexity bound under strong convexity.

B.5 Phase‑Transition Bound (Network Percolation)

Theorem B.5 (Critical coupling threshold).
Model the colony as a multi‑layer network G = (V, E_{\text{phys}} \cup E_{\text{inst}}) where nodes are subsystems and edges denote physical or institutional dependencies. Assume institutional edges fail independently with probability p. There exists a threshold p_c such that if p < p_c, a giant connected cooperative component persists with high probability; if p > p_c, the network fragments and systemic collapse occurs.

Proof sketch. This follows from percolation theory on random graphs. Using the configuration model or an Erdős–Rényi approximation, compute p_c = 1/\langle k \rangle where \langle k \rangle is the mean degree. The candidate derives an early‑warning signal from the spectral gap \lambda_2 of the Laplacian: as p \nearrow p_c, \lambda_2 \to 0. The candidate must calibrate this signal with simulation data and provide a probabilistic bound on false positives. ∎

B.6 Compositionality (Wiring Diagrams)

Theorem B.6 (Composition of safety contracts).
Let each subsystem i (life‑support, economy, law) be modelled as an open dynamical system with typed input/output ports. A safety contract \mathcal{C}_i is a relation \mathcal{C}_i \subseteq \text{In}_i \times \text{Out}_i such that any signal pair (u, y) satisfying \mathcal{C}_i guarantees the subsystem never violates its local safety property. Given a wiring diagram \mathcal{W} specifying how outputs feed into inputs, the composite system satisfies the contract

\mathcal{C} = \left\{ (u_{\text{ext}}, y_{\text{ext}}) \;\middle|\; \exists \text{ internal flows satisfying all } \mathcal{C}_i \text{ and the wiring equations} \right\}.

If each \mathcal{C}_i is satisfied by the component, then the composite contract \mathcal{C} implies the global safety invariant.

Proof sketch. The proof is a direct application of the compositionality theorem for relational contracts in the algebra of open systems (Fong & Spivak, 2019). The candidate must formalise the wiring diagram in a categorical language (e.g. as an operad) and machine‑check the compositionality proof. This reduces global verification to local obligations. ∎

B.7 Delay‑Margin Guarantee

Theorem B.7 (Stability under Earth–Moon communication delay).
Consider the linearised governance feedback loop

\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = A \mathbf{x}(t) + B K \mathbf{x}(t - \tau),

where \tau is the one‑way communication delay. If there exists positive definite matrices P, Q and a constant \tau_{\max} > 0 such that the Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional

V(\mathbf{x}_t) = \mathbf{x}(t)^\top P \mathbf{x}(t) + \int_{-\tau}^0 \mathbf{x}(t+\theta)^\top Q \mathbf{x}(t+\theta) \, d\theta

satisfies \dot{V}(t) < 0 for all \tau \in [0, \tau_{\max}], then the closed‑loop system is asymptotically stable for any delay less than \tau_{\max}.

Proof sketch. Apply the Lyapunov–Krasovskii stability theorem. Derive linear matrix inequalities (LMIs) whose feasibility guarantees the negativity of \dot{V}. Solve the LMIs numerically to obtain the maximum allowable delay. The candidate must linearise the institutional dynamics around the desired equilibrium and verify the LMIs using a tool such as YALMIP or CVX. The obtained \tau_{\max} must be at least the Earth–Moon one‑way light‑time (1.3 s). ∎

