Appendix F. Model Reduction and Digital Twin Interface

F.1 Reduced‑Order Institutional Model

The full simulation generates a high‑dimensional state trajectory \mathbf{X} \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times n} where n is the number of continuous state variables (resource levels, sentiment indices, etc.). Using Proper Orthogonal Decomposition (POD), we compute the singular value decomposition of the snapshot matrix \mathbf{X} = U \Sigma V^\top and truncate to the first r modes, r \ll n, capturing e.g. 99% of the variance. The reduced‑order dynamics are given by a projected ODE:

\dot{\mathbf{z}}(t) = U_r^\top f( U_r \mathbf{z}(t), u(t) ),

where f is the original institutional dynamics and U_r are the retained POD modes.

Error Bound: The truncation error in the state is bounded by the sum of the discarded singular values: \| \mathbf{X} - U_r U_r^\top \mathbf{X} \|_F^2 = \sum_{i=r+1}^n \sigma_i^2. The candidate must prove that this error does not compromise the verification of the safety invariant (i.e. the reduced‑order model is conservative with respect to the forbidden state).

F.2 Digital Twin Interface Specification

The reduced‑order institutional model is packaged as a containerised microservice with the following REST API:

· GET /health — returns current institutional health metrics (resource margins, conflict index, phase‑transition early‑warning signal).
· POST /step — advances the model by one time step given external inputs (e.g. life‑support sensor readings), returns updated metrics.
· POST /inject_shock — injects a predefined stressor (supply interruption, personnel loss) and returns predicted recovery time.

All endpoints are served with formal bounds on response latency to ensure co‑simulation with the physical Digital Twin can proceed in real time.

